In most cases, <ESC> aborts an action before it's finished.  I.e., you
press the wrong key, a prompt comes up, and you hit escape.  But there
are 2 important cases where this does not work:

- You have typed some characters but the game hasn't acted on them yet.
  This typically happens when you are moving around quickly, and is
  especially noticeable on levels with lots of monsters, where response
  is really slow.  The prime example must be fighting the giant eels on
  the castle level:  you press a key, nothing happens, so you figure you
  didn't press it hard enough and do it again.  5 seconds later the eel
  moves, another 5 seconds later your character falls in the moat.

- You asked to wait or search N times, but the key bounced and so you
  are waiting 500 times instead of 50.  Or you asked to wait, but you
  can see some nasties coming toward you and want to run instead.

How difficult would it be for the game to look ahead in the keyboard
input, and abort waits, searches, and keyboard reads if an escape was
present in the buffer?

This would make the game more playable without removing any fairness,
since slow system response, keyboard bounce, and too-large counts on
commands should not (IMHO) be factors in a characters death.  Besides,
with any other counted commands (like zapping a wand), there is a way to
abort.

